<Base slug="algorithms/mix-and-variety/" title="Safe, stretch, wild and variety" description="How The Cousin keeps three suggestions from being three copies of the same idea: a safe bet, a stretch and a wild card.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">04 · The algorithms · Stage 3</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>Three good ideas, not the same idea three times.</h1>
    <div class="headrow" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">
      <p class="lede muted">Ranking alone puts the three most similar places on top. A cousin would offer one sure thing, one a little new, and sometimes one surprise.</p>
      <Status s="tested" />
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">In plain words</p>
    <div class="pair three">
      <div class="term magnet"><h2 class="h3">Safe</h2><p>The best match for this person tonight. If they only read one line, this one should be right.</p></div>
      <div class="term gold"><h2 class="h3">Stretch</h2><p>Still a strong match, but a little newer: a cuisine they’ve tried once, a part of town they rarely visit.</p></div>
      <div class="term fence"><h2 class="h3">Wild</h2><p>Something new to them, picked only from ideas that still pass every fence and fit their taste. Never random.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Watch it work</p>
    <Video v="V04" />
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">On the example</p>
    <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Dinner</th><th>Why it’s here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Safe</td><td>Kaveri thali place</td><td>South Indian, calm, loved last month</td></tr><tr><td>Stretch</td><td>A Gujarati thali house</td><td>Veg and calm, a cuisine tried once</td></tr><tr><td>Wild</td><td>A rooftop millet café</td><td>New idea, still veg, under ₹500 and on the way</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">The wild slot is not on every reply. How often it appears, and which kinds of surprise, is learned per person: see <a href={url('algorithms/wild-cards/')}>wild cards</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Asking for more</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="h3">Three is the default, not the limit.</p>
      <p>A reply holds three ideas because a cousin gives a few good options, not a page of search results. If you want more, say so. The ranked shortlist behind those three already exists (about 40 places passed the fences), so more ideas cost almost nothing and still obey every rule.</p>
      <div class="tbl" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>You say</th><th>What you get</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>“Show me more”</td><td>The next three: a new safe, stretch and wild</td><td>The mix runs again on the same shortlist, leaving out what you’ve already seen</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Give me 10 options”</td><td>A short list of up to 10, best first</td><td>List mode: the mix still spreads the picks, every fact is printed by code</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“More like the second one”</td><td>Three close to the stretch pick</td><td>That pick becomes tonight’s taste; ranking runs again</td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Any others?” when nothing is left</td><td>A clear “that’s all that fits”, plus the one rule to relax</td><td>The fences never loosen on their own</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p class="muted">A list stops at about 10. Past that, choosing gets harder, not easier, so the cousin asks what to change instead.</p>
    </div>
    <Status s="designed" />
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">What we learned</p>
    <div class="prose"><p><b>Variety is what makes people come back.</b> In the simulations, suggesting what is popular repeated a recently seen place in 11 to 17% of plans. With the mix and the rest period, that fell to 6 to 7%, while the first suggestion was accepted more often, not less.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">For engineers</p>
    <details class="eng">
      <summary>The re-ranking formula</summary>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="muted">Numbers below are proposed starting values, to be tuned.</p>
        <pre class="code">pick 1 = argmax score(x)
pick k = argmax  λ·score(x) − (1−λ)·max_sim(x, picked)     # MMR
λ = 0.7 for stretch;  wild drawn from novelty-weighted pool</pre>
        <p>This is maximal marginal relevance (MMR), the standard way search engines diversify results. Similarity is cosine over the tag vector today, and over embeddings once we have them.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
